{
  "sourceId": "2018-P2-Q01",
  "source": "<a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://uat-wp.s3.eu-west-2.amazonaws.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/05/07125404/TMUA-2018-paper-2.pdf#page=3\">TMUA 2018 · Paper 2 · Question 1</a> · <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://uat-wp.s3.eu-west-2.amazonaws.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/05/07125406/TMUA-2018-paper-2-worked-answers.pdf#page=3\">Official worked answer</a>",
  "label": "TMUA 2018 · Paper 2 · Question 1",
  "lead": "<img class=\"source-question\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" aria-label=\"Enlarge question\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"TMUA 2018 · Paper 2 · Question 1 — Evaluate f prime at 4 for f(x)=(x^3-4x)/(2sqrt(x)).\">",
  "options": [
    "A",
    "B",
    "C",
    "D",
    "E",
    "F"
  ],
  "correct": "C",
  "hints": [
    {
      "title": "Simplify the radical quotient",
      "body": "<p>Use <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></msup></mrow></math> and split the fraction. Both numerator terms must be divided by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><msqrt><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math>.</p>",
      "recap": "Rewrite the function as powers before applying calculus.",
      "pitfall": "The second term simplifies using <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math>.",
      "pause": "Find the coefficients and exponents of the simplified function.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b1-l18",
          "reminder": "Rewrite the function as powers before applying calculus."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Differentiate before substituting",
      "body": "<p>The function is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>5</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></msup></mrow></math>. Use the power rule to find f′(x), then evaluate at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math>.</p>",
      "recap": "A derivative value requires the derivative formula, not the original function value.",
      "pitfall": "Substituting first produces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, which is not the requested gradient.",
      "pause": "Find f′(x) in two terms.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b1-l18",
          "reminder": "A derivative value requires the derivative formula, not the original function value."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "solution": "<p>For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>x</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><msqrt><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>5</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></msup></mrow></math>. Differentiate to get <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>5</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></msup></mrow></math>.</p><p>At <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msup><mn>4</mn><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>4</mn><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>. Therefore <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>5</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo></mrow></math><strong>9.5, option C.</strong></p>",
